; The development probes.
;
; These are not the engine and are not on any gate's path: the conformance runner
; starts `bin/jlreq_ocaml_engine.exe` and nothing else, and `just ocaml-gate` never
; builds anything here on purpose. They exist because a milestone is debugged one
; number at a time, and `DIFF <case-id>` is not a number.
;
;   just diffcase quick-start/two-lines     # one case, field by field
;   just census spacing                     # every class pair, both engines
;
; Unlike the engine, a probe may read a file at run time -- the suite, a generated
; census -- because nobody hands it a request over a pipe from an undisclosed
; working directory. It may also start a subprocess, which it does through
; `Sys.command` and two temporary files rather than `Unix.open_process`: this tree's
; OCaml depends on the compiler and dune and nothing else, and `unix` would be the
; first library outside that promise.
;
; Two `executable` stanzas rather than one `executables`, because dune links every
; module of a stanza into every executable it names -- and OCaml runs a linked
; module's top-level effects. `census.exe` would run `diffcase.ml`'s `let ()` on the
; way to its own. The `modules` field keeps each probe to itself.

(executable
 (name diffcase)
 (modules diffcase)
 (libraries jlreq jlreq_proto)
 (flags
  (:standard -w +a-4-40-41-42-44-45-70 -strict-sequence -strict-formats -safe-string)))

(executable
 (name census)
 (modules census)
 (libraries jlreq jlreq_proto)
 (flags
  (:standard -w +a-4-40-41-42-44-45-70 -strict-sequence -strict-formats -safe-string)))
